#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The scoreboard: every measured subject of the jk loop, banked, banded and ranked against its target.

    bench/scoreboard --tier fast               # count and paired-wall rows, judged and banked
    bench/scoreboard --tier night              # then every absolute wall behind the quiet gate, the
                                               # night queue, the docs tables and the morning report
    bench/scoreboard --dim counts --runs 5     # one dimension
    bench/scoreboard --dim paired --baseline B # the candidate ($JK_BIN) against jk binary B
    bench/scoreboard --subject 'inner.noop.*'  # rows whose subject matches the glob
    bench/scoreboard --no-bank                 # judge only; scoreboard.toml is not rewritten
    bench/scoreboard --gaps                    # banked rows ranked by distance to target, nothing measured
    bench/scoreboard --render                  # docs tables from the banked rows, nothing measured
    bench/scoreboard --selftest                # the band arithmetic

A row is one (dimension, subject, project, tool, kind). Kinds:

  count          work done, read from counters that load does not move: tasks run, bytes and calls
                 written, files rewritten, bytes read, output size. Each row's runs must agree within
                 the row's band, or the row is `unstable` and neither banked nor judged.
  paired-wall    candidate against baseline run interleaved; the median pair ratio with its CI. It
                 fails when the CI's lower bound is above 1 + band, and records today's ratio.
  absolute-wall  published seconds and peak RSS, behind the quiet gate and the calibration run; a
                 row taken on a busy or drifting host is `noisy` and neither banked nor judged.

Every value is lower-is-better. A jk row worse than its banked value by more than its band fails the
run (exit 1) and prints the commit range since the line was banked. A row better by more than the
improvement threshold is rewritten in scoreboard.toml: an improvement is banked, never a failure.
A row the file has never seen is seeded at its measured value. Another tool's row records what
that tool does today. Rows from another host are kept and never compared.

Dimensions live in bench/scoreboard.d/<name>.py (files starting with `_` are shared helpers), each
with TIERS (the tiers it runs in), an optional KIND = "absolute-wall", measure(ctx) -> list of row
dicts and an optional render(board, host). Results land in $SCOREBOARD_HOME/results/<stamp>/rows.json
(default $JK_BENCH_HOME/scoreboard). docs/perf/README.md is the reference.
"""

